        <body>
            <div xml:id="d346">

                <pb facs="0515" n="416" sameAs="frus:frus1936v02/pg416"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">860c.51/1029a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Chargé in Poland</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Nielsen</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-06-27T13:00:00-04:00">June 27, 1936—1 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>33. According to press despatch from London dated June 26, published in <hi
                        rendition="#italic">New York Times</hi> June 27, the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Financial News</hi> of London has stated that it understood from
                    authoritative sources that Polish Government intended to continue in full all
                    payments due in Britain (presumably <pb facs="0516" n="417" xml:id="pg417"/> on
                    Polish bonds floated in Great Britain) as the balance of trade between Great
                    Britain and Poland is favorable to Poland which is not the case with the United
                    States.</p>
                <p>Please cable Department whether there is any basis for this report.</p>
                <p>Department desires to be kept promptly informed, briefly by cable and fully by
                    mail, with regard to any developments indicating any difference in the treatment
                    of British and American holders of Polish bonds. Department desires particularly
                    to be advised of any action taken by the British Government formally or
                    informally in connection with this matter.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Carr</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
